ePiX provides the following abstract Shape objects:
* Line
* Segment
* Plane
* Circle
* Sphere

Shape objects can be intersected (as appropriate), translated, scaled,
and drawn. The Line, Segment, Plane, and Sphere classes are implemented
as non-zero Vectors, while a Circle consists of a Vector and a radius.

Recall that an ePiX Vector consists of a Position p=(p1,p2,p3) and a
triple v=(v1,v2,v3). 

* The associated Line is the set p + tv, t real, which passes through
  p in the direction v.

* The head and tail of a Vector define a Segment, namely the set of
  Positions on the Line above for which 0 <= t <= 1.

* The associated plane is the set of Positions x such that (x-p)|v=0, 
  namely the plane through p that is perpendicular to v.

* A Vector and a double define the circle of given radius whose center
  is the tail, and that lies in the plane perpendicular to the Vector.

* A Vector defines the sphere centered at the tail and containing the
  head.


Affine Operations:

Translation of a Shape is translation of the defining Vector. Scaling
is only meaningful for Segments, Circles, and Spheres, and constitutes
expansion ("homothety") about the center (for Circles and Spheres) or
expansion about the midpoint for Segments.


Intersections:

Shapes have "generic" intersections that satisfy the following table:

       | Line    Segment   Plane  Circle   Sphere
--------------------------------------------------
Line   | Empty   Empty     Posn   Segment  Segment
--------------------------------------------------
Segment|         Empty     Posn    N/A     N/A
--------------------------------------------------
Plane  |                   Line   Segment  Circle
--------------------------------------------------
Circle |                          Segment  Segment
--------------------------------------------------
Sphere |                                   Circle

Non-generic (or null) intersections cause exceptions to be thrown. The
following exceptions are provided:

NON_COPLANAR (Lines, Segments, and Circles)
PARALLEL     (Lines, Segments, and Planes)
DISJOINT     (Segment intersected with other types)
COINCIDENT   (All types)
TANGENT      (Circles and Spheres intersected with other types)
CONCENTRIC   (Circles and Spheres -- means one object is inside the
              other, but not necessarily truly concentric)
SEPARATED    (Circles and Spheres -- DISJOINT but not CONCENTRIC)
